  "task": "Triage whether a ~33% failure rate on direct OpenAI API calls (not Bedrock) is caused by the provider (OpenAI) or by the customer's own side (network/egress, quotas/rate limits, credentials, deploys/config, code).",
  "context": "The customer runs an agent that calls the OpenAI API directly for inference. Since this morning (2026-11-24, UTC) roughly one third of calls fail. Their own service-level metrics look normal. They want a provider-vs-us verdict, with evidence, done ad hoc (this is NOT a formal Investigation). Agent space account 012345678901_1, region us-east-1, but workloads may live in other associated accounts/regions - use the associations and topology to find them. First locate the agent workload (Lambda, ECS/EKS, EC2, etc.) and its log groups by looking for code/config/log references to api.openai.com, openai, or OPENAI_API_KEY.",
  "resources": ["Agent space 012345678901_1 / us-east-1 and any associated accounts", "Workloads calling api.openai.com", "NAT gateways / VPC egress / Route 53 resolver / security groups for that workload", "Secrets Manager / SSM parameters holding the OpenAI key (read metadata only, never read or print secret values)"],
  "timeframe": "Failure window: 2026-11-24T00:00:00Z to 2026-11-24T18:57:00Z (find the precise onset time). Baseline: 2026-11-23T00:00:00Z to 2026-11-24T00:00:00Z.",
  "criteria": "1) Determine the precise onset time and whether the failure rate is constant or bursty. 2) Classify failed calls by error type from app logs: HTTP 429 (rate limit vs insufficient_quota - note these are customer-account-side), 401/403 (key/org/billing), 5xx/503/529 and 'server_error' or 'overloaded' (provider-side), timeouts / connection reset / DNS / TLS errors (network path or provider), and any OpenAI request IDs or x-ratelimit headers. 3) Check whether failures are concentrated by model, endpoint, region, AZ, instance/pod, API key, or request size/token count - concentration suggests the customer side, uniform spread suggests provider. 4) Check network path health: NAT gateway metrics (ErrorPortAllocation, PacketsDropCount, ConnectionAttemptCount vs ConnectionEstablishedCount), VPC flow logs rejects, DNS resolution failures, Lambda/ECS throttles or timeouts. 5) Check changes in the window: deployments, config/env var changes, secret rotations, SDK/library version bumps, CloudTrail changes to networking/security groups/NACLs/routes, scaling events that increased concurrency. 6) Check whether the app's own retry/timeout settings mask or amplify failures (why 'our metrics look normal'; e.g. errors swallowed, fallbacks, or metrics counting only successful requests). 7) Give a verdict: provider-side, customer-side, or inconclusive, with a confidence level, the evidence for each, what would change the verdict, and concrete next steps (e.g. check status.openai.com, OpenAI usage/limits dashboard, specific request IDs to give OpenAI support). Be explicit that you cannot see OpenAI's own status page. Do not make any mutating changes."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Triage OpenAI call failures: logs, network, changes"}], "created_at": null}

